From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Subject: [PATCH] memories: a write mask with a ragged last lane
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A memory's write mask lanes had to split its word evenly, so pulp's
tc_sram, whose byte enables cover a word that is not whole bytes, could
not become a macro: cva6's I-cache tag RAM is 25 bits with 4 byte
enables, 8, 8, 8 and 1 bit wide.

A memory may now declare mask_lane_width: every lane is that wide but
the last, which holds the rest (lanes * width >= bits > (lanes - 1) *
width). FakeRAM builds the mask bus as before, one pin per lane, and
its Verilog writes each lane's own bit range. Without a width the lanes
split the word evenly, as before, and an uneven split is refused as
before.

Carried in bazel-orfs, not upstreamed; retires at a //:bump onto an
ORFS that has it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
---
